  "narrative": "In the 2020s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture continued to thrive, building upon the rich heritage of their ancestors. The natural hair movement, which originated in the 1960s, persisted as a powerful symbol of self-love and acceptance, encouraging individuals to celebrate their unique textures and styles. Hairstyles such as afros, cornrows, and box braids remained staples of African diasporic culture, not only as protective styles but also as expressions of identity and community. Dreadlocks, too, were a popular choice among some women, who saw them as a way to reconnect with their natural hair's true potential. Unfortunately, documentation from this era is limited, but it is clear that these hairstyles continued to play a vital role in the lives of Black and African women, serving as both functional and symbolic representations of their beauty and resilience.",
